{
  "info": {
    "_postman_id": "b6f2a1a0-6e3e-4b1a-9e7a-4f2c8f1c9d10",
    "name": "Crypto Tracker API",
    "description": "Stage 0 (base setup) + Stage 2 (api-rest) + Stage 3 (auth-firebase) endpoints. Mirrors requests.http.\n\nRun `npm run dev` first. Stage 2 requests need a seeded catalog (`npm run seed:coins`) and at least one poll-prices run (`npm run job:poll-prices` or `npm run dev:worker`) for non-empty responses.\n\nStage 3 (auth): the old `X-Admin-Key` header is gone. Every authenticated request needs a Firebase ID token. The 'Auth (Firebase)' folder gets you one straight from Postman against the local Auth emulator (`firebase emulators:start --only auth`), no CLI script required:\n1. Run 'Sign up (create test user)' once per test account — it also stores the token in `idToken`.\n2. Call 'Me > Get my profile' once with that token to provision the Mongo profile.\n3. For admin requests, repeat steps 1-2 with the `adminEmail`/`adminPassword` variables to get `adminIdToken`, then run `npm run user:set-role -- --email {{adminEmail}} --role admin` from a terminal (Postman can create the Firebase account and provision the Mongo profile, but promoting to admin is a deliberate out-of-band step — see design.md).\n\nTo test against a real Firebase project instead of the emulator, change `authApiBaseUrl` to `https://identitytoolkit.googleapis.com/v1` and set `firebaseWebApiKey` to your project's real Web API Key (Firebase console -> Project settings -> General).",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
